#{[17592186287959 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2x & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2x & 5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4-15)i-(2x-6x)j+(5x-4x)k \\\\  & =⟨-11,4x,x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(4x)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ \\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121} & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ 17{x}^{2}+121 & =393 \\\\ 17{x}^{2} & =272 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 4.$"] [17592186287960 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((5)(0)-(3)(4))i-((1)(0)-(2)(4))j+((1)(3)-(2)(5))k \\\\  & =(0-12)i-(0-8)j+(3-10)k \\\\  & =-12i+8j-7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287961 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((2)(-3)-(2)(0))i-((-2)(-3)-(0)(0))j+((-2)(2)-(0)(2))k \\\\  & =(-6-0)i-(6-0)j+(-4-0)k \\\\  & =-6i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287972 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)-3(3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =4-3-16 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287973 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 3)+2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-12-2+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287974 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a\\cdot (2b\\times (-c)) & =8 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =8 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287975 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}b\\times 3c) & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $c$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (c\\times b) & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $c$ and $a$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287976 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a\\cdot (6b\\times c) & =90 \\\\ 3\\cdot 6\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =90 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290547 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$"] [17592186290552 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).$"] [17592186290553 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{\\left(-\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$"] [17592186290554 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"] [17592186292832 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 0)i-(6\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 5)j+(6\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =(6-0)i-(-18-0)j+(0-(-10))k \\\\  & =6i+18j+10k \\\\  & =⟨6,18,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292833 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2x & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 5 \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(-2x-10x)j+(0-6x)k \\\\  & =⟨3,12x,-6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(12x)^{2}+(-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+144{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ \\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ 180{x}^{2}+9 & =909 \\\\ 180{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"] [17592186292837 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$"] [17592186292839 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},10,-10⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3})+0\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =3+0+20 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292840 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))i-((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))j+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-4i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4+2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292915 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -3 & \\le 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 3 \\\\ -3-2 & \\le 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\le 3-2 \\\\ -5 & \\le x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 5 & \\text{ }\\ge -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -5 \\\\ -5 & \\le -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 5 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le 5.$"] [17592186295036 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 1)i-(5\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot (-2))j+(5\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =(8+3)i-(20-6)j+(5+4)k \\\\  & =11\\,i-14\\,j+9\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295037 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(x-2)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =⟨2,2-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4-4x+{x}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+24} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+24 & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=2.$"] [17592186295221 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-5))i-(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =(8-5)i-(6+2)j+(-15-8)k \\\\  & =3i-8j-23k \\\\  & =⟨3,-8,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295222 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 0)i-(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 1)j+(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i+\\sqrt{3}j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\sqrt{3},-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295223 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((0)(-3)-(1)(2))i-(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(-3)-(1)(1))j+(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(2)-(0)(1))k \\\\  & =(0-2)i-(-2-1)j+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =-2i+3j+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,\\frac{4}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295227 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)(6)-(1)(5))i-((3)(6)-(1)(-2))j+((3)(5)-(-4)(-2))k \\\\  & =(-24-5)i-(18+2)j+(15-8)k \\\\  & =-29\\,i-20\\,j+7\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295228 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-(-5)(4))i-(1\\cdot 1-(-5)(-3))j+(1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =(2+20)i-(1-15)j+(4+6)k \\\\  & =22\\,i+14\\,j+10\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295229 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot -1)i-(-6\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)j+(-6\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =(15+2)i-(-30-8)j+(6-12)k \\\\  & =17\\,i+38\\,j-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295230 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-7)(-6)-(4)(3))i-((2)(-6)-(4)(5))j+((2)(3)-(-7)(5))k \\\\  & =(42-12)i-(-12-20)j+(6+35)k \\\\  & =30\\,i+32\\,j+41\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295231 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0)(-2)-(3)(2)i-(-4)(-2)-(3)(1)j+(-4)(2)-(0)(1)k \\\\  & =(0-6)i-(8-3)j+(-8-0)k \\\\  & =-6\\,i-5\\,j-8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295235 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2x & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ -2x & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 3 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(12-0)i-(8x+2x)j+(0+6x)k \\\\  & =⟨12,-10x,6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}+(-10x)^{2}+(6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+100{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ \\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144} & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ 136{x}^{2}+144 & =280 \\\\ 136{x}^{2} & =136 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-1,$ $x=1.$"] [17592186295236 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 0 & 1 \\\\ -x & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0-2)i-(0+x)j+(2x+0)k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-x,2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4} & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4 & =29 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"] [17592186295237 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 3 & 0 \\\\ -x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(x+0)j+(0+3x)k \\\\  & =⟨3,-x,3x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(3x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+9 & =49 \\\\ 10{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=2.$"] [17592186295238 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(3x-1)j+(0-1)k \\\\  & =⟨3,1-3x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(1-3x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11} & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-6x+11 & =10 \\\\ (3x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186295239 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2x-2)j+(0-1)k \\\\  & =⟨2,2-2x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-2x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(4{x}^{2}-8x+4)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+9 & =5 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (2x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1.$"] [17592186295240 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4-0)i-(x-5)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =⟨4,5-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(5-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+(25-10x+{x}^{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57} & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+57 & =33 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+24 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =4,\\,x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=4,$ $x=6.$"] [17592186295241 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(4x-12)j+(0-3)k \\\\  & =⟨2,12-4x,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(12-4x)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(144-96x+16{x}^{2})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ \\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157} & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x+157 & =157 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x & =0 \\\\ 16x(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=6.$"] [17592186299580 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨\\sqrt{7},-2,5⟩\\cdot ⟨\\sqrt{7},3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{7})+(-2)(3)+(5)(-1) \\\\  & =7-6-5 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299581 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-\\sqrt{8},\\,4,\\,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨\\sqrt{8},\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{8})(\\sqrt{8})+(4)(-1)+(-3)(2) \\\\  & =-8-4-6 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299582 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨0,\\,5,\\,\\sqrt{11}⟩\\cdot ⟨7,\\,-2,\\,\\sqrt{11}⟩ \\\\  & =(0)(7)+(5)(-2)+(\\sqrt{11})(\\sqrt{11}) \\\\  & =0-10+11 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299583 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 1)\\,-\\,(-1)\\,(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\,+\\,3\\,(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2\\,(19)\\,-\\,(-1)\\,(2)\\,+\\,3\\,(8) \\\\  & =38+2+24 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299584 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-1))\\,-\\,2\\,(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\,+\\,3\\,(4\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =1\\,(1)\\,-\\,2\\,(22)\\,+\\,3\\,(-4) \\\\  & =1-44-12 \\\\  & =-55.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299585 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\,(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-1))\\,-\\,2\\,(3\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 4)\\,+\\,5\\,(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =0(-2)\\,-\\,2\\,(-6)\\,+\\,5\\,(-7) \\\\  & =0+12-35 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299586 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 6)\\,-\\,2\\,(4\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 5)\\,+\\,3\\,(4\\cdot 6-0\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =1\\,(12)\\,-\\,2\\,(14)\\,+\\,3\\,(24) \\\\  & =12-28+72 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299587 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))\\,-\\,1\\,(1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)\\,+\\,3\\,(1\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =2\\,(8)\\,-\\,1\\,(2)\\,+\\,3\\,(-25) \\\\  & =16-2-75 \\\\  & =-61.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299588 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(1\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 2)\\,-\\,0\\,(3\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 4)\\,+\\,2\\,(3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =1\\,(5)\\,-\\,0\\,(15)\\,+\\,2\\,(2) \\\\  & =5+0+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299589 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2)\\,-\\,1\\,(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)\\,+\\,0\\,(0\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =2\\,(-5)\\,-\\,1\\,(-20)\\,+\\,0\\,(-15) \\\\  & =-10+20+0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299590 "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\,((-1)(-3)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\,-\\,(-2)\\,(4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\,+\\,3\\,(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(7)\\,-\\,(-2)\\,(-10)\\,+\\,3\\,(9) \\\\  & =-7-20+27 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299594 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-1,6⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =8-3-6 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299922 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302048 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{6} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1 \\\\ y & =-6(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.$"] [17592186302049 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}\\right)-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$"] [17592186302103 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$"] [17592186302104 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(x+3) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(y-1) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"] [17592186302105 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.\n$$"] [17592186302106 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-13 & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-13)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & =1 \\\\ {(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2}=49.$"] [17592186302364 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-6\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ }\\ge -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -3 \\\\ -3 & \\le -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 3 \\\\ -3 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le y\\le 3.$"] [17592186302365 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{9}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{5}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$"] [17592186302369 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4+1 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 4+1 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le x\\le 5.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{7}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.$"] [17592186302370 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\le -\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{7}{3}\\le \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{29}{6} & \\le y\\le \\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{29}{6}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{6}.$"] [17592186314703 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{t}^{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323976 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329713 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=1.\n$$"] [17592186329714 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186329716 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=1.\n$$"] [17592186329718 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"] [17592186335809 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\n$$"] [17592186335810 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t & ={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$"] [17592186335811 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186335815 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+{sin}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+{sin}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186335816 "First, we factor ${sin}^{3}⁡x$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{5}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x={sin}^{3}⁡x\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{3}⁡x\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) & ={sin}^{3}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{sin}^{5}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x={sin}^{3}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186335817 "First, we factor ${cos}^{2}⁡x$  from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{4}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+{sin}^{2}⁡x & ={cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{4}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$"] [17592186335818 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{3}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{3}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"] [17592186335819 "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186335820 "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186335821 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$"] [17592186341845 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186341846 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\n$$"] [17592186341847 "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$"] [17592186341976 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-3))i-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 3)j+(2\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3i-3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-3,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341977 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =14i+8j-5k \\\\  & =⟨14,8,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨14,8,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 14+4\\cdot 8+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341978 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 1)i-((-2)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)j+((-2)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-4i+8j+k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,8,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
